Go.dot patch 0002 for Tracktion Engine: a launched clip's speed, which can change while it plays.

A clip in a launcher slot plays its source one-for-one with the Edit's beats:
every block its wave node is told which stretch of the file the block's beats
cover and is moved there, so a speed given to the resampler alone would be
undone at the next block. And its slot ends it at "launch + length" on the beat
grid. Go.dot's media cues need a speed from nought to twenty that moves while
they sound (namespace draft section 22).

This adds LaunchHandle::SpeedSource, an interface the host implements and sets
on a clip's handle, asked on the audio thread two questions it answers from
nothing but its arguments and what the host has placed: how far beyond
one-for-one a clip launched at a beat has played, and when it will have played
a length of its source. Tracktion keeps no answer between blocks, so a graph
rebuilt in the middle of a change of speed needs nothing carried across it.

- WaveNodeRealTime reads the stretch of source the speed says; Tracktion's own
  per-block ratio then drives its resampler or its time-stretcher.
- SlotControlNode ends a clip where its SOURCE has played the clip's length,
  and a slot rebuilt while its clip plays carries on playing it: a fresh slot
  told its reader the playhead had jumped, which at any ratio but one is a
  jump of a fraction of a sample - a faint tick at every arm of another cue.
- A resampled read is faded to silence as its speed nears nought (full at 0.05,
  silent at 0.01, Tracktion's own tape-stop and slowest-clip numbers) and not
  read at all below 0.01; a stretched read at nought is fed nothing and keeps
  sounding what the stretcher holds, a freeze.
- TimeStretchReader is told nought as a very large stretch rather than a
  division by nought, and primes at speed one - its prime counts output frames
  as input frames, which holds only at one - AIMED for the speed it will play
  at. A stretcher's lag, in input samples, is its input latency plus the speed
  times its output latency (Signalsmith's own outputSeekLength), so one primed
  at one and played at another speed is (1 - speed) x its output latency out
  of place in its source: 95 ms early at half the speed, 47 ms late at twice.
  So TimeStretcher reports its output latency apart (nought for a stretcher
  that does not know it, which is primed as before), and the prime aims that
  much earlier, or later above one.
- Both stretch readers prime again at their first read when the prime they
  were built with fell short. They are built with the graph, while the file
  cache may still be loading the file, and a real-time cache read does not
  wait: the prime read silence, and every stretched clip opened on a whole
  latency of nothing - 150 ms at 48 kHz - with its file's first moments lost.
  A stretcher whose file was there primes once, as before. Both this and the
  aim were found by M14, 2026-09-29.
- And the plain stretch reader keeps time through a file-cache miss, as a
  resampled read does: a real-time cache read does not wait, and fails
  whenever the cache's own thread holds the reader, so it feeds silence for
  what the cache could not hand over and carries on. It used to give up the
  block and hold its place - every later moment a chunk late, and a prime of
  dozens of reads failing whole and beginning again the next block (10 to
  60 ms late on the CI runners). Only the prime it is built with stays strict.
- The Lagrange resampler takes a read too large for the scratch pool or the
  file cache in halves, and is given the EXACT ratio, putting back whatever
  the interpolator did not consume. It was fed a whole number of frames a
  block, so at any ratio that is not one - a speed that moves, a file at
  another sample rate - the file position drifted until setPosition's
  one-sample tolerance reset the interpolator: a small click every few blocks.
  And setPosition moves the file only on a jump, judged against where the
  reader exactly is - moved to the rounded request every block, it handed the
  interpolator a frame twice or skipped one, and judged against the file's
  whole-frame position it reset whenever a moving ratio moved the part of a
  frame the interpolator had pending.

With no speed source, or one at exactly one, every read is the same doubles as
before. Each file it touches carries a notice that Go.dot modified it (GPL-3,
section 5(a)).

Applied by Go.dot's build, cmake/WfgTracktionPatches.cmake, in the order
patches/tracktion_engine/series gives; refreshed from the working tree with
scripts/te-patches.py refresh, which keeps this header. Against Tracktion
Engine 13b5132 (2026-09-23). Namespace draft section 22.3, decisions DQ-DX.

